{
"An argument for someone else has to start from what that person already accepts. They saw logic as a way of meeting another mind.": "83419bd8a292",
"Aristotle invented formal logic in the Prior Analytics, and his definition is still standard. A syllogism is discourse in which, certain things being stated, something other than what is stated follows of necessity from their being so. Its basic pattern is the first figure: If A is predicated of all B, and B of all C, A must be predicated of all C. The Buddhist model fits it exactly, with produced things as the middle term. The important difference is that Aristotle studied validity in abstraction from content. Letters stand for the terms, and the conclusion follows whatever they are. Examples play no part in the proof. The Indian logicians never separated form from content in this way. An argument was always about something, addressed to someone, and judged by whether its reason really had the three marks in the world. The Greeks produced a theory of validity, and the Indians produced a theory of good reasons.": "9f3727ea27fb",
"Aristotle studied valid form. The Indian logicians studied good reasons, which includes checking the world.": "4f98b4e36d05",
"Buddhist logicians required every argument to give an example and a counterexample.": "d6d1475030cd",
"Indian philosophy was argued in public. Teachers of rival schools debated before kings and assemblies, and losing could cost a school its patrons. The Nyāya school set out a standard form for a proof in five parts. The hill has fire. Because it has smoke. Wherever there is smoke there is fire, as in a kitchen. The hill has smoke. So the hill has fire. Buddhist logicians shortened it. The manual of Dignāgas school that Xuanzang translated into Chinese names three members, the thesis, the reason, and the example, and gives a model built on a Buddhist doctrine. Sound is impermanent is the thesis. Because it is produced is the reason. Then comes the example in two forms: Whatever is produced is seen to be impermanent, like a pot, and whatever is permanent is seen not to be produced, like space.": "51eaef2962c4",
"Mill raised the oldest objection to the syllogism in its sharpest form. Take All men are mortal; the Duke of Wellington is a man; therefore the Duke of Wellington is mortal. If the first premise is known to be true, the dukes mortality must already have been known, so the argument seems to beg the question. Mills answer was that the general premise is not really the ground of the conclusion. The ground is the many observed deaths of other people, and the general statement is a memorandum of them. Not only may we reason from particulars to particulars without passing through generals, but we perpetually do so reason. The child who has burned his fingers avoids the candle without ever having formed the maxim that fire burns. This is very close to the Indian picture, in which the example, the kitchen or the pot, is part of the evidence and is stated in the argument itself. Mills rules for finding causes, by checking agreement and difference among cases, also match the second and third marks: the reason found with the property, and absent where the property is absent.": "1727ab6686a8",
"On form the closest parallel is Aristotle. The Buddhist model argument, once its example is removed, is a valid syllogism in the first figure, and scholars have long noted the match. On what makes an argument good, Mill is closer. He and the Indian logicians agree that a general rule earns its place only through observed cases, and that a careful reasoner must look for cases where the reason appears without the property. On the practice of argument Toulmin is closest. He and they treat an argument as a move in an exchange, made to persuade someone who may push back.": "c131d5a0df94",
"One of their textbook fallacies is My mother is a barren woman. The sentence refutes itself.": "ac037f5581b6",
"The British philosopher Stephen Toulmin complained that formal logic had little to say about how arguments work in courts, laboratories, and committees. He proposed a layout with more parts than the syllogism: a claim, the data offered for it, a warrant that licenses the step from data to claim, the backing that supports the warrant, a qualifier saying how strong the claim is, and the rebuttals under which it would fail. The Indian three-membered argument maps onto this closely. The thesis is the claim, the reason is the data, the general statement is the warrant, the example is its backing, and the dissimilar example and the catalog of fallacies anticipate the rebuttals. Toulmin was trying to recover what the Indian logicians had never lost, logic as the practice of giving reasons to someone who may disagree. He drew his models from law. Theirs came from the debating hall.": "01b4ccbc9e0d",
"The counterexample line is a built-in check against seeing only what agrees with you.": "2f24f3bd79c3",
"The first further step is the dissimilar example. Aristotles form does not ask for it, and modern logic counts it as redundant, since it follows from the general premise. The Indian logicians required it to be stated, and the reason is practical. A person who asserts that everything produced is impermanent is easily convinced by the cases that agree with him. The third mark forces him to turn and look at the other side: is there anything permanent that is produced? Western philosophy reached the same lesson later and stated it as a principle of science. A general claim is tested by the search for a counterexample, and Popper built a philosophy of science on it. The Buddhist logicians had written the search into the form of every argument. It is a discipline against confirmation bias, the habit of looking only for cases that agree.": "21db5d649ae8",
"The heart of the system is a test for a good reason, which the manual calls its three marks. The reason has three marks. What are the three? It is present throughout the subject, it is definitely present in similar cases, and it is absent from all dissimilar cases. Applied to the model: sound is produced, so the reason is present in the subject. Pots and other produced things are impermanent, so it is found in similar cases. Nothing permanent, such as space, is produced, so it is absent from the dissimilar cases. The two examples are there to show the second and third marks at work. The positive example shows the reason together with what is to be proved. The negative example shows that wherever the property to be proved is absent, the reason is absent too.": "7b34c01337a3",
"The likeness ends at the purpose. Aristotle wanted a science of demonstration, Mill a logic for the natural sciences, and Toulmin a logic for practical fields. The Buddhist logicians wanted these things too, and their model argument shows what else they wanted. Its conclusion is that sound is impermanent, one of the three marks of existence, and the argument is a step toward the insight that frees. Dharmakīrti said that the purpose of the whole inquiry into means of knowledge is to know what to take up and what to avoid. The manual distinguishes inference for oneself, which is the private act of understanding, from inference for others, which is the spoken proof. A teacher who argues well for others is offering another person the chance to see what he has seen.": "3743788d0fa6",
"The manual spends most of its length on the ways arguments go wrong, and some of its examples are memorable. A thesis can contradict perception, as in sound is not heard, or contradict itself, as in my mother is a barren woman. A reason can fail to be established, as when one argues that sound is impermanent because it is seen by the eye. It can be inconclusive, as when one argues that sound is permanent because it is knowable, since permanent and impermanent things are both knowable. It can even prove the opposite of what was intended. The list was meant for use. A debater who knew it could find the fault in an opponents proof, and a student who knew it could find the fault in his own.": "35d9d61ca66a",
"The second step is the listener. The manual opens by saying that proofs and refutations serve to bring understanding to others, and that perception and inference serve ones own understanding. An argument for others must start from premises the other person already accepts. One of the listed faults is a reason accepted by only one party, and another is a thesis that both parties already grant, which proves nothing. Logic here is a form of courtesy. One meets the other person where he stands and walks with him, step by step, to where one wants him to see. The tradition of Buddhist debate in Tibetan monasteries still trains monks this way, with a challenger and a defender and a fixed form for each move. When the logic came to China with Xuanzang it was studied for a few generations and then largely set aside, and Chinese Buddhism turned to schools that distrusted argument. Something was lost. The Indian logicians had shown that rigorous reasoning and the path to release could be pursued by the same person, in the same act.": "dfdc0643f504",
"Their model was: sound is impermanent, because it is made, like a pot; and nothing permanent, like space, is made.": "8858b5676089",
"Westerners often assume that logic is a Greek invention and that Asian traditions relied on intuition or authority. The Buddhist logicians are the best correction available, and they are easy to present. Write the model argument on a board in plain English: sound is impermanent, because it is made; whatever is made is impermanent, like a pot; whatever is permanent is not made, like space. Ask the audience what the last line adds. Someone will say it checks for exceptions, and that is the point of the lesson. Then mention the list of fallacies, with the barren mother as the example everyone remembers. End with the purpose. These logicians argued carefully because the conclusions concerned how to live, and they thought that a careless argument on such matters does harm.": "6b0bd22435d9"
}
